From 80d7cd123454a7ba50709db2e6949421c9f7469d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 19:36:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/dvbridge: make the picture that Kodi draws over only
 where the drawing reaches

A frame of native Dolby Vision with something drawn over it (the GUI, text
subtitles) is composed: in the source transport, the transport is decoded
into a 3840x2160 RGBA32F picture (pass 3), Kodi blends its GUI over that,
and the packer reads that picture for every pixel to tell where something
was drawn (its alpha is not 2) and converts those back (pass 4). Where
nothing is drawn, the packer takes the codes of the transport and nothing
of that picture. So for two lines of subtitles the frame wrote and read
the whole 132 MB picture, twice: on the integrated Radeon of a Ryzen 9
7950X3D, a frame with GUI elements took 24 to 31 ms of GPU time where the
same frame drawn directly takes 13.4.

Now, where the caller says before the frame what will be drawn over it,
and that is a GUI texture composited over the whole raster as Kodi's
CompositeGui() composites it (dvbridge_render_set_drawn(),
dvbridge_source_drawn()), the packer first finds the tiles of 16 x 16
pixels of the raster that it may reach: the tiles of the GUI texture with
an alpha above 0 (pass 6, in two draws of 4 x 4 texels each), and the
raster tiles whose pixels sample those texels with the composite's
bilinear filter, its rows the other way up, four texels more on every side
(pass 7: medium-precision texture coordinates are off by up to a texel at
3840). Pass 3 then draws only those tiles, and pass 4 draws every tile,
each knowing from its vertices whether something may be drawn over it,
and reads the picture only there. Both are a quad for each tile with the
same fragment shaders as before; a tile not drawn has no fragments (no
discard and no lookup for each pixel, which cost more than they saved).
Outside those tiles nothing is drawn over the picture, and pass 4 takes the
codes of the transport, as it did where it read an alpha of 2: the same
bytes, whatever the texture holds there from frames before. The measurement
of what is drawn over the bars (pass 5) reads only those tiles too.
Anything else that is to be drawn over the picture, or read from it,
asks for the rest of it first (dvbridge_render_complete(): pass 3 for the
other tiles). What draws over it without that is not shown (pass 4 reads
nothing there), never as garbage. Without what will be drawn (0, the
converted transport, a renderer for HDR10, the reference of a check, the
warm-up), and where passes 6 and 7 cannot be made, everything is as before:
the triangle over the whole raster.

Measured with cover_test.c, the frames of a profile 7 FEL stream with Kodi's
own composite (its shaders from the tree, its LUTs, projection and blend
factors) over them, from GUI textures of 3840x2160, 1920x1080, 2560x1440
and 1280x720 with nine drawings (empty, a window over a dimmed screen, an
OSD, two lines of subtitles, a seek bar, text, the faintest opacity in a
corner and over everything, random texels) and single texels at the
boundaries of the tiles, the picture's texture filled with something else
before each frame:
- on Mesa's software OpenGL ES (zink on lavapipe), and on the integrated
  Radeon and the RTX 4080 SUPER of a test PC through Mesa's OpenGL on
  Direct3D 12 in WSL: the bytes of every frame (106 for each GPU and
  picture) are those it has as before, also with PQ subtitles drawn
  anywhere after the picture is made whole; the picture made whole is the
  whole picture, to the bit;
- eight mutations each make it fail (no margin, the picture read in every
  tile, the tiles drawn the other way round, the rows of the GUI not
  reversed, a faint alpha not counted, the measurement of the bars over
  every tile, the rest made in the tiles, the quads one tile off);
- the time of a frame with GUI elements over the video (cover_test
  guitime: the frame as Kodi draws it, the GPU's work to its end, nothing
  read back; medians of 9), 1.0 RC2 against this commit (with the
  measurement of the bars block by block before it), the way that waits
  (the edges measured) / a frame ahead (every edge with bars). On the
  integrated Radeon through Direct3D 12: two lines of subtitles 29.8/24.2
  ms before, 17.9/16.4 now; the seek bar 30.5/24.2, 18.2/16.4; an empty
  GUI 29.7/24.0, 16.9/16.1; an OSD 42.6/25.9, 31.0/21.6; a window over a
  dimmed screen 62.2/38.3, 49.6/38.5 (the frame drawn directly: 13.2). On
  the RTX 4080 SUPER: 1.5 to 9.3 / 0.9 to 1.5 before, 1.2 to 2.2 / 0.8
  to 1.6 now (directly: 0.8). On lavapipe, where a quad for each tile
  costs more than one triangle: 70.9 to 185.3 / 53.7 to 75.6 before, 59.2
  to 91.3 / 57.0 to 89.3 now (directly: 38.4).

Not verified: the times with the drivers that LibreELEC runs (radeonsi,
i915, nvidia-drm), and the frames on a display.
